{
  "query": "The retail site at scrumtuous.com is building a marketplace that stores structured data for customers, items, shopping carts, and orders with complex relationships between entities. The checkout flow must support atomic multi statement transactions with full ACID guarantees and the system processes about 90 purchases per minute while keeping related records consistent. Which database type best matches these requirements?",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Document NoSQL database",
      "explanation": "This option stores semi structured documents such as JSON and allows flexible schemas.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Column family NoSQL database",
      "explanation": "This option organizes data by column families and is tuned for wide sparse tables and high write throughput.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Relational SQL database",
      "explanation": "This option uses normalized tables with relationships and supports multi row transactions with strong consistency and ACID guarantees.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Key value NoSQL database",
      "explanation": "This option stores records as key and value pairs and focuses on very fast simple lookups with limited query patterns.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct option is <b>Relational SQL database</b>.</p><p>This choice fits a marketplace with many related entities because it models relationships cleanly with normalized schemas and foreign keys. It supports atomic multi statement transactions with full ACID guarantees so the checkout flow can update customers, carts, inventory, and orders as one consistent unit. The expected throughput of about 90 purchases per minute is well within the capacity of a transactional engine and benefits from strong consistency and referential integrity. On Google Cloud you would typically use Cloud SQL or AlloyDB for traditional deployments or Cloud Spanner if you also require global scale and very high availability while keeping ACID properties.</p><p><i>Document NoSQL database</i> is a poor fit because it favors denormalized documents and does not natively provide SQL joins or strict relational constraints. While limited transactions may exist in some offerings, they are not designed for multi statement SQL workflows across many related tables which makes cross entity consistency harder.</p><p><i>Column family NoSQL database</i> is optimized for large scale and wide column access patterns with single row atomicity. It lacks multi row ACID transactions and SQL joins which are essential for maintaining consistency across customers, items, carts, and orders.</p><p><i>Key value NoSQL database</i> excels at simple key based lookups and high throughput for independent records. It does not provide relational joins or multi statement transactional guarantees across multiple records, so it cannot reliably enforce consistency across the related entities in this checkout flow.</p>",
